# image2blender

A plugin for **Claude Code**, **Codex CLI**, and **Cursor** that turns a
reference image into a **quality-gated, procedural 3D model in a live Blender
scene** — via Blender MCP. One shared skill, bundled three ways so each host
loads it as a native plugin.

Give it a photo, product shot, concept art, or room interior and it will:

1. **Gate the input** — probe the image, judge suitability, classify
   object / character / scene and complexity.
2. **Write a spec first** — a 3×3-grid detail inventory, a component tree,
   material estimates, and per-pass acceptance criteria, validated by a
   deterministic script before any build is allowed.
3. **Build in locked passes** — `blockout → structure → form → materials →
   detail → lighting`, executed as bpy code inside your running Blender.
4. **Review every pass** — render fixed angles, compare against the reference
   with vision, record an explicit verdict (`continue` / `refine-spec` /
   `refine-code` / `request-input` / `stop`), with a bounded repair budget so
   it never loops silently.

Geometry stays procedural (primitives, modifiers, curves) so the result is
editable and animation-ready; Poly Haven textures/HDRIs are used where they
help. The staged, review-gated methodology is adapted from
[img2threejs](https://github.com/hoainho/img2threejs) (Apache-2.0) — this
plugin ports it from generated Three.js code to direct Blender scene
construction over MCP.

## Requirements

- One of: **Claude Code**, **Codex CLI**, or **Cursor** (all three support
  plugins that bundle a skill + an MCP server)
- **Blender 3.0+**, running, with the [blender-mcp addon](https://github.com/ahujasid/blender-mcp)
  installed and connected (see below)
- **uv** — install with the official installer (not `pip install uv`):
  ```bash
  curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh
  ```
  Every host launches the same bundled MCP server with `uvx blender-mcp`.

## Install

<details open>
<summary><strong>Claude Code</strong></summary>

From the marketplace repo:

```bash
claude
# then inside Claude Code:
#   /plugin marketplace add ssota-labs/image2blender
#   /plugin install image2blender@image2blender
```

Or for local development / from a checkout:

```bash
claude --plugin-dir /path/to/image2blender
```

Invoke with `/image2blender:image-to-blender`, or just describe the task —
the skill's description triggers it automatically.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Codex CLI</strong></summary>

```bash
codex
# then inside Codex:
#   /plugins
#   → add marketplace source ssota-labs/image2blender
#   → install image2blender
```

`plugin.json` for Codex lives at [.codex-plugin/plugin.json](.codex-plugin/plugin.json)
and points at the same `skills/` directory Claude Code uses — no separate
skill content to maintain. Start a new session after installing so the
bundled `blender` MCP server and skill are picked up.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Cursor</strong></summary>

Cursor's plugin marketplace install flow is evolving fastest of the three;
as of this writing, local development is the more reliable path:

```bash
# symlink this repo into Cursor's local plugin directory
mkdir -p ~/.cursor/plugins
ln -s /path/to/image2blender ~/.cursor/plugins/image2blender
```

Then restart Cursor (hot-reload isn't available yet for plugins) and enable
it from the Customize page. `plugin.json` for Cursor lives at
[.cursor-plugin/plugin.json](.cursor-plugin/plugin.json), again pointing at
the shared `skills/` directory.
</details>

### Blender-side setup (once, any host)

1. Download `addon.py` from [ahujasid/blender-mcp](https://github.com/ahujasid/blender-mcp)
2. Blender → Edit → Preferences → Add-ons → Install… → select `addon.py` →
   enable **Interface: Blender MCP**
3. Each session: press `N` in the 3D viewport → **BlenderMCP** tab →
   **Connect to MCP server**

## Use

With Blender running and connected, just give your coding agent an image:

> "여기 이 머그컵 사진, Blender로 모델링해줘" / "Recreate this product shot in Blender"

The skill's description is written to auto-trigger on this kind of request in
all three hosts; Claude Code additionally supports the explicit
`/image2blender:image-to-blender` invocation.

Each run writes its working artifacts to `image2blender-runs/<subject>/` in
your project — `spec.json` (the reconstruction contract, including the review
history) and `renders/` (per-pass review captures) — so runs are auditable and
resumable, regardless of which host produced them.

Exact MCP tool names differ slightly by host (e.g. `execute_blender_code` is
namespaced differently in each) — see
[references/setup.md](skills/image-to-blender/references/setup.md#tool-names-by-host)
for the mapping; the skill itself doesn't require knowing this, it's only
useful for manual debugging.

## Using the official Blender Lab MCP instead

The skill's core loop only needs an `execute_blender_code` tool, so it also
works with the [official Blender MCP](https://projects.blender.org/lab/blender_mcp)
maintained by the Blender team (add-on via blender.org extensions; configure
the server yourself following your host's own MCP setup — `claude mcp add`,
Codex's `[mcp_servers]` in `config.toml`, or Cursor's `mcp.json`). Differences:
no Poly Haven tools (the skill falls back to procedural textures) and no
viewport screenshot tool (the skill's review loop renders to files
regardless). Run only one Blender-side MCP add-on at a time — both bind port
9876.

## Safety notes

- `execute_blender_code` runs arbitrary Python in your Blender session.
  **Save your work** before letting a build pass run on a non-empty file; the
  skill also asks first and confines itself to its own collection.
- Timeouts on heavy operations are handled by chunking; if Blender is busy
  (e.g. rendering), calls will fail until it's idle.

